  "worked_solution": "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions.",
